<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.1.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02094</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.1.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ab1e35a7-e8fd-4cd7-bbc4-a747e2cab7dc</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="43cb649a-6876-421a-abfd-da32b1877199" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--q1-->
  <exercise data-content-id="ddd8d7a4-d23a-4d5a-b35d-49e504aa12a4" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="28 feet" data-solution-options="[&quot;18 feet&quot;, &quot;22 feet&quot;, &quot;28 feet&quot;, &quot;34 feet&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Use the following scenario to answer questions 1 – 3.</para>
      <para>A water balloon is launched by a catapult into the air. The water balloon is launched from 4 feet above the ground, with an initial vertical velocity of 40 feet per second.</para>
      <para>The function \(h(t)=-16t^2 + 40t + 4\) models the height of the water balloon over the ground, in feet, \(t\) seconds after launch.</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="A DOWNWARD PARABOLA. X-AXIS LABEL IS TIME IN SECONDS AND Y-AXIS LABEL IS HEIGHT IN FEET. THE GRAPH PASSES THROUGH THE POINTS (0, 4), (0.5, 20), (1, 28), (1.5, 28), (2, 20), AND (2.5, 4)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/796834755c9625082b6b6d76fe2c1c69b585dd60"/></media></figure></para>
      <para>What is the height of the water balloon 1 second after launch?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.1.1.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="1be11c84-cac7-4c2b-a1b7-9167d49e00a8" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="Yes" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Will the water balloon still be in the air 2 seconds after the launch?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.1.1.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="a533105e-d0af-4da7-967b-ccef854cc82c" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="2.6 seconds" data-solution-options="[&quot;1.2 seconds&quot;, &quot;1.8 seconds&quot;, &quot;2.1 seconds&quot;, &quot;2.6 seconds&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Using the graph, estimate when the water balloon hit the ground.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.1.1.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <exercise data-content-id="4422f95e-f1f8-48d2-bf0d-cfade1823c86" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="The perimeter of the framed picture is greater than 42 inches." data-solution-options="[&quot;The perimeter of the picture is 40 inches.&quot;, &quot;The perimeter of the framed picture is 42 inches.&quot;, &quot;The area of the framed picture is 148 square inches.&quot;, &quot;The perimeter of the framed picture is greater than 42 inches.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Use the following scenario to answer questions 4 – 5.</para>
      <blockquote>Suppose you want to frame a different picture that measures 12 inches by 9 inches. The frame is created from 46 square inches of framing material. The frame must be uniform in width, and all of the framing material must be used.</blockquote>
      <para>Which of the following is true about the situation described?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.1.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q5-->
  <exercise data-content-id="1ae10f49-546a-48b8-bb26-ddbd7b9a1c3d" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="154 square inches" data-solution-options="[&quot;46 square inches&quot;, &quot;108 square inches&quot;, &quot;154 square inches&quot;, &quot;176 square inches&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>What is the total area of the framed picture?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.1.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q6-->
  <exercise data-content-id="6b5eaf55-8eee-4a1a-a8ee-c3934e06334e" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="110 square inches" data-solution-options="[&quot;34 square inches&quot;, &quot;72 square inches&quot;, &quot;110 square inches&quot;, &quot;134 square inches&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Use the following scenario to answer questions 6 – 8.</para>
      <blockquote>Suppose you want to frame a different picture that measures 9 inches by 8 inches. The frame is created from 38 square inches of framing material. The frame must be uniform in width, and all of the framing material must be used. Let \(x\) represent the thickness of the frame.</blockquote>
      <para>What is the total area of the framed picture?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.1.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q7-->
  <exercise data-content-id="6a449a14-2cd1-4fc4-bbfd-f71f176ea2bf" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(4x^2 + 34x + 72 = 110\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(x^2 + 34x = 38\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(4x^2 + 34x = 72\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(x^2 + 34x + 72 = 110\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(4x^2 + 34x + 72 = 110\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Write an equation to represent the relationship between the measurements of the picture and of the frame and the area of the framed picture.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.1.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q8-->
  <exercise data-content-id="39bb92af-ed5e-43a7-a333-cfa5c679ca6f" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="The solution represents the thickness of the frame when all of the framing material is used." data-solution-options="[&quot;The solution represents the area of the framing material that should be used. &quot;, &quot;The solution represents the thickness of the frame when all of the framing material is used.&quot;, &quot;The solution represents the perimeter of the frame when all of the framing material is used.&quot;, &quot;The solution represents the total area of the framed picture.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>What does the solution to the equation in the previous question represent in this situation?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.1.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q9-->
  <exercise data-content-id="a4e79ad6-377a-4762-bfed-389be7b9a423" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="5 inches and 7 inches" data-solution-options="[&quot;2 inches and 2 inches&quot;, &quot;2 inches and 5 inches&quot;, &quot;2 inches and 7 inches&quot;, &quot;5 inches and 7 inches&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Use the following scenario to answer questions 9 – 10.</para>
      <blockquote>A framed picture has a total area \(y\), in square inches. The thickness of the frame is represented by \(x\), in inches. The equation \(y = (2x + 5)(2x + 7)\) relates these two variables.</blockquote>
      <para>What are the width and length of the picture without the frame?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 8.1.4.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q10-->
  <exercise data-content-id="abad243c-0cd5-40c2-84e1-bb24cf570b68" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(y − 35\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(y − 35\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(x + y\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(x + 35\\)&quot;, &quot;35&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which of the following represents the area of the frame?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para> Try again. Revisit activity 8.1.4.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Do not edit below this line-->
  <solution> </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response"> </div>
</div></section>
</content>
